// expect: answers f = 42
// why: a parameter is captured like any other local, and shared rather than copied
// from: design/lang/0002 tier two
//
// A captured local becomes a cell where it is declared. A parameter has none — it arrives already in
// its slot — so its cell is built at function entry from the incoming value. The distinction is
// invisible in the language and it is two different pieces of the emitter, which is why it has a case
// of its own: the local half worked for a while when this did not.
//
// The write is what makes it a capture by reference rather than a copy of the argument. `f` takes no
// parameter itself and calls one that does, so the value under test is written here rather than
// depending on how the runner calls an exported function that has one.
i32 twice(i32 start) {
  fn[void()] bump = () => { start = start + 20; };
  bump();
  bump();
  return start;
}

export i32 f() { return twice(2); }
